    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "GPT-5.6 Sol on wolfram2002-ch10-24: partially-resolvable, resolvability 0.28";
    dc:description "Existing benchmark results and method documentation could test restricted method classes. No comprehensive public census spans human perception and all practical mathematical, statistical, and computational analyses, and broad conclusions would depend heavily on taxonomy choices.";
    dc:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/concept/dataset-resolvability-0.2.0>;
    dc:type "resolution-route/mixed";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Proposed test with existing data: Define a representative inventory of deployed perceptual and analytical methods, then classify the structural regularities each can detect under standardized benchmarks. The claim is supported only if nearly all methods detect repetition or nesting but systematically fail on agreed higher-order structures.";
    prov:wasAttributedTo <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/agent/delphisci-resolvability-assessor-gpt-5.6-sol-medium>;
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/assessmentType> "dataset-resolvability/0.2.0";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/evidenceAgainst> "Method selection and the definition of higher-order regularity are contestable.", "The quantifiers 'essentially all' and 'almost nothing more complex' lack fixed boundaries.";
